Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct,joint,medqa,"A junior orthopaedic surgery resident is completing a carpal tunnel repair with the department chairman as the attending physician. During the case, the resident inadvertently cuts a flexor tendon. The tendon is repaired without complication. The attending tells the resident that the patient will do fine, and there is no need to report this minor complication that will not harm the patient, as he does not want to make the patient worry unnecessarily. He tells the resident to leave this complication out of the operative report. Which of the following is the correct next action for the resident to take?","[{'key': 'A', 'value': 'Disclose the error to the patient but leave it out of the operative report'}, {'key': 'B', 'value': 'Disclose the error to the patient and put it in the operative report'}, {'key': 'C', 'value': 'Tell the attending that he cannot fail to disclose this mistake'}, {'key': 'D', 'value': 'Report the physician to the ethics committee'}, {'key': 'E', 'value': 'Refuse to dictate the operative report'}]",B,Disclose the error to the patient and put it in the operative report,"The resident has a duty to inform the patient about any complications that occurred during the procedure, even if the attending physician does not want to disclose it. This is a fundamental principle of patient autonomy and informed consent. The operative report is a medical record that documents the events of the surgery, and it is essential to accurately reflect the events that occurred. Failing to disclose the error in the operative report could lead to a lack of transparency and potentially compromise the patient's trust in the healthcare team. The resident should disclose the error to the patient and include it in the operative report to maintain transparency and adhere to medical ethics.","Answer: B. Disclose the error to the patient and put it in the operative report
