Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct,joint,medqa,A 67-year-old man with transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder comes to the physician because of a 2-day history of ringing sensation in his ear. He received this first course of neoadjuvant chemotherapy 1 week ago. Pure tone audiometry shows a sensorineural hearing loss of 45 dB. The expected beneficial effect of the drug that caused this patient's symptoms is most likely due to which of the following actions?,"[{'key': 'A', 'value': 'Inhibition of thymidine synthesis'}, {'key': 'B', 'value': 'Inhibition of proteasome'}, {'key': 'C', 'value': 'Hyperstabilization of microtubules'}, {'key': 'D', 'value': 'Generation of free radicals'}, {'key': 'E', 'value': 'Cross-linking of DNA'}]",E,Cross-linking of DNA,"The patient's symptoms are likely due to cisplatin, a chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder. Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapeutic agent that works by cross-linking DNA, thereby inhibiting DNA replication and transcription. This mechanism of action is responsible for its antineoplastic effects. However, cisplatin is also known to cause ototoxicity, particularly sensorineural hearing loss, as a side effect. The ototoxicity is thought to be due to the formation of reactive oxygen species that damage the hair cells in the inner ear. The beneficial effect of cisplatin in treating cancer is due to its ability to cross-link DNA, which is option E.","Answer: E. Cross-linking of DNA
